Let  $f:[0,1)\to (0,1)$  a continous onto (surjective) function. 

a) Prove that, for all  $a\in(0,1)$ , the function  $f_a:(a,1)\to (0,1)$ , given by  $f_a(x) = f(x)$ , for all  $x\in(a,1)$  is onto;
b) Give an example of such a function.

solution_md:
a) Note that  $[0,a]$  is compact, so there exist  $x_1 ,x_2$  with  $0< x_1= \min_{x\in [0,a]} f(x), 1>x_2 =\max_{x\in [a,b]} f(x).$  Let  $y\in (0,1)$ , then we can choose  $0<y_1 < \min(x_1 , y)$  and  $1>y_2 > \max(x_2 ,y)$  and by the surjectivity of  $f$ , it must attain the values  $y_1, y_2$  in the interval  $(a,1)$ , and so by the IIntermediate Value Theorem also the value  $y.$ b) The following piecewise-linear function should also work. Define
\begin{align*}
f\colon [0,1) &\rightarrow (-1,1)
x &\mapsto \begin{cases} \frac{(-1)^{n}(1-\frac{1}{n})}{2^{-n+1}}(x-a_{n-1}) \;\; \text{if}\, x\in [a_{n-1}, a_{n-1}+ 2^{-n+1}]
(-1)^{n}(1-\frac{1}{n})- \frac{(-1)^{n}(1-\frac{1}{n})}{2^{-n+1}}(x-a_{n-1}-2^{-n+1}) \;\; \text{if}\, x\in [a_{n-1}+ 2^{-n+1}, a_{n}],
\end{cases}
\end{align*}
where  $a_0=0, a_k =\sum_{i=1}^{k} 2^{-i}$  for  $k>1.$  Since  $\bigcup_{n\geq 1} [a_{n-1} , a_n]=[0,1)$ , this function has the correct domain. It takes the value  $(-1)^{n}(1-\frac{1}{n})$  at  $x= a_{n-1}+ 2^{-n+1}$  and is hence surjective. It also takes the value  $0$  at  $x=a_n$  and so we see that  $f$  is actually continuous. Therefore, the function  $g\colon [0,1) \rightarrow (0,1), x\mapsto \frac{1}{2}(f(x)+1)$  has all desired properties.

