<details><summary>Solution</summary>If any of  $ a,b,c$  are equal to zero, then the inequality follows, so assume all three complex numbers are nonzero.  Then the condition on  $ a,b,c$  can be divided by  $ |abc|$  to obtain  $ \frac{a}{|a|} + \frac{b}{|b|} + \frac{c}{|c|} = 0$ .  Note that all numbers of the form  $ w = \frac{z}{|z|}$  are on the unit circle, and  $ w$  has the same argument as  $ z$ .  Thus, all the condition is saying is that if we draw three rays from the origin through  $ a,b,$  and  $ c$  on the complex plane, then the points where these rays intersect the unit circle form a triangle whose centroid is the origin.  Because the circumcenter of this triangle concides with the centroid (both are the origin), we can conclude that this triangle is equilateral.  (Is there a synthetic solution from this point on?) Thus, we can say that  $ a = Ae^{i \theta}$ ,  $ b = Be^{i (\theta + 120^\circ)}$ , and  $ c = Ce^{i(\theta+240^\circ)}$ , where  $ A,B,$  and  $ C$  are the magnitudes of  $ a,b,$  and  $ c$ , respectively.

Now, because  $ a,b,c$  are assumed to be nonzero, we can divide  $ |(a-b)(b-c)(c-a)| \geq 3\sqrt 3 |abc|$  
by  $ |abc|$  to get  $ \left|\frac{(a-b)(b-c)(c-a)}{abc}\right| \ge 3\sqrt{3}$ .  We will start with the LHS.

\begin{eqnarray*}
\left|\frac{(a-b)(b-c)(c-a)}{abc} \right|  &=& \left|\frac{a^2c+b^2a+c^2b-a^2b-b^2c-c^2a}{abc}\right| 
&=& \left|\frac{a}{b}+\frac{b}{c}+\frac{c}{a} - \frac{a}{c} - \frac{b}{a} - \frac{c}{b}\right| 
&=& \left|\frac{A e^{i \theta}}{B e^{i (\theta +120^\circ)}} + \frac{B e^{i (\theta +120^\circ)}}{C e^{i (\theta + 240^\circ)}} + \frac{C e^{i (\theta + 240^\circ)}}{A e^{i \theta}}
- \left( \frac{A e^{i \theta}}{C e^{i(\theta + 240^\circ) }} + \frac{B e^{i (\theta+120^\circ)}}{A e^{i \theta}} + \frac{C e^{i(\theta+ 240^\circ)}}{B e^{i (\theta+120^\circ)}} \right) \right|
&=& \left| \left( \frac{A}{B} + \frac{B}{C} + \frac{C}{A} \right) e^{i (-120^\circ)} - \left(  \frac{A}{C} + \frac{B}{A} + \frac{C}{B} \right) e^{i 120^\circ} \right| 
&=& \left| \left( \frac{A}{B} + \frac{B}{C} + \frac{C}{A} \right) \left(\frac{1}{2} - \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \right) - \left(  \frac{A}{C} + \frac{B}{A} + \frac{C}{B} \right) \left( \frac{1}{2} + \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \right) \right| 
\end{eqnarray*}

At this point, we let  $ x = \frac{A}{B} + \frac{B}{C} + \frac{C}{A}$  and  $ y =  \frac{A}{C} + \frac{B}{A} + \frac{C}{B}$ , for simplicity.

\begin{eqnarray*}
 \left| x \left(\frac{1}{2} - \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \right) - y \left( \frac{1}{2} + \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \right) \right|  &=& 
\frac{1}{2} |(x-y) - i\sqrt{3} (x+y) | 
&=& \frac{1}{2} \sqrt{ (x-y)^2 + ( \sqrt{3}(x+y))^2 } 
&=& \frac{1}{2} \sqrt{4x^2+4xy+4y^2} 
&=& \sqrt{x^2+xy+y^2} 
\end{eqnarray*}

Now, we have  $ (x-y)^2 \ge 0$ , so  $ x^2 + y^2 \ge 2xy$ .  Thus,

\[ \sqrt{x^2 + xy + y^2} \ge \sqrt{3xy}.\]

Now, we will show that  $ xy \ge 9$ .  Let  $ \frac{A}{B} = p$ ,  $ \frac{B}{C}=q$ , and  $ \frac{C}{A}=r$ , so  $ p+q+r =x$ ,  $ pq+pr+qr = y$ , and  $ pqr = 1$ .  
Then \[ xy=(p+q+r)(pq+pr+qr) = 3pqr + \sum_{sym} p^2q = 3 + \sum_{sym} p^2q \ge 9,\]
where the last step follows from muirhead, or AM-GM:  $ \frac{1}{6} (p^2q+p^2r+q^2p+q^2r + r^2p+r^2q) \ge \sqrt[6]{p^6q^6r^6}$ , so  $ \sum_{sym} p^2q \ge 6$ .  

Thus,  $ \sqrt{3xy} \ge \sqrt{27} = 3\sqrt{3}$  and we are done. [</details>

