 $x=a+\lambda y=a+\lambda(b+\lambda z)=a+\lambda(b+\lambda(c+\lambda x))=a+b\lambda+c\lambda^2+\lambda^3 x\implies x=\frac{a+b\lambda+c\lambda^2}{1-\lambda^3}$ So the system has solution  $x=\frac{a+b\lambda+c\lambda^2}{1-\lambda^3}$ ,  $y=\frac{b+c\lambda+a\lambda^2}{1-\lambda^3}$ , and  $z=\frac{c+a\lambda+b\lambda^2}{1-\lambda^3}$ \begin{align*}
x^2+y^2+z^2
&=\frac{(a+b\lambda+c\lambda^2)^2+(b+c\lambda+a\lambda^2)^2+(c+a\lambda+b\lambda^2)^2}{(1-\lambda^3)^2}
&=\frac{(a^2+b^2+c^2)(1+\lambda^2+\lambda^4)+2(ab+ac+bc)(\lambda+\lambda^2+\lambda^3)}{(1-\lambda^3)^2}
&=\frac{(a^2+b^2+c^2)(1+\lambda+\lambda^2)(1-\lambda+\lambda^2)+2\lambda(ab+ac+bc)(1+\lambda+\lambda^2)}{(1-\lambda^3)^2}
&=\frac{(a^2+b^2+c^2)(1-\lambda+\lambda^2)+2\lambda(ab+ac+bc)}{(1-\lambda)^2(1+\lambda+\lambda^2)}
&\leq\frac{(a^2+b^2+c^2)(1-\lambda+\lambda^2)+2\lambda(a^2+b^2+c^2)}{(1-\lambda)^2(1+\lambda+\lambda^2)}\quad (\text{Cauchy–Schwarz})
&=\frac{(1-\lambda+\lambda^2)+2\lambda}{(1-\lambda)^2(1+\lambda+\lambda^2)}
&=\frac{1}{(1-\lambda)^2}
\end{align*}

The minimum of  $f(x) = \max\{|\sin(x)|, |\sin(x+1)|\}$  occurs when  $-\sin(x) = \sin(x+1)$  for all  $x \in \mathbb{R}$ , or: $-\sin(x) = \sin(x)\cos(1)+\cos(x)\sin(1)$ ;

or  $-\sin(x)[1+\cos(1)]=\sin(1)\sqrt{1-\sin^{2}(x)}$ ;

or  $\sin^{2}(x)[1+\cos(1)]^2 = \sin^{2}(1)[1-\sin^{2}(x)]$ ;

or  $\sin^{2}(x)\left[\frac{1+\cos(1)}{\sin(1)}\right]^2 = 1-\sin^{2}(x)$ ;

or  $\left[\frac{1+\cos(1)}{\sin(1)}\right]^2 + 1=\frac{1}{\sin^{2}(x)}$ ;

or  $|\sin(x)| = \frac{\sin(1)}{\sqrt{2[1+\cos(1)]}} = 0.47945 > \frac{1}{3}$ .

Let  $r \in \mathbb{R}^{+}$  and  $a=x+yi$  for  $x,y \in \mathbb{R}$ .  These lead to: $\sum_{k=1}^{n} \frac{|a-r\omega^{k}|^2}{|a|^2+r^2} = \sum_{k=1}^{n} \frac{|(x+yi) - r[\cos(2k\pi/n) +i\sin(2k\pi/n)]|^2}{x^2+y^2+r^2}$ ;

or  $\sum_{k=1}^{n} \frac{|[x- r[\cos(2k\pi/n)] +i[y-r\sin(2k\pi/n)]|^2}{x^2+y^2+r^2}$ ;

or  $\sum_{k=1}^{n} \frac{[x- r[\cos(2k\pi/n)]^2 +[y-r\sin(2k\pi/n)]^2}{x^2+y^2+r^2}$ ;

or  $\sum_{k=1}^{n} 1 -\frac{2r}{x^2+y^2+r^2}[x\cos(2k\pi/n) +y\sin(2k\pi/n)]$ ;

or  $n - \frac{2r}{x^2+y^2+r^2}\sum_{k=1}^{n}[x\cos(2k\pi/n) +y\sin(2k\pi/n)]$ ;

or  $n - \frac{2r}{x^2+y^2+r^2}\left[x\sum_{k=1}^{n}\cos(2k\pi/n) +y\sum_{k=1}^{n}\sin(2k\pi/n)\right]$ .

By the Roots of Unity, we have:  $\sum_{k=1}^{n}\cos(2k\pi/n) = \sum_{k=1}^{n}\sin(2k\pi/n)=0$ ;

and consequently  $\sum_{k=1}^{n} \frac{|a-r\omega^{k}|^2}{|a|^2+r^2} = \textcolor{red}{n}$  for all integers  $n \ge 3$ .  This geometrically represents the sum of distances from the origin of the Complex Plane to the vertices of the regular polygon formed by the  $n-$ th Roots of Unity.

Let  $l(x,y,z)=x+y+z+\sqrt{x^2+y^2}+\sqrt{y^2+z^2}+\sqrt{z^2+x^2}$  be the sum of the lengths of this right tetrahedron and  $v(x,y,z) = \frac{1}{6}xyz=a^3$  be its volume for all  $x,y,z,a \in \mathbb{R}^{+}$ .  Taking the Lagrange Multiplier  $\nabla l = \gamma \nabla v$  yields the set of equations: $1 + \frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}} + \frac{x}{\sqrt{z^2+x^2}} = \gamma \cdot \frac{yz}{6}$  (i); $1 + \frac{y}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}} + \frac{y}{\sqrt{y^2+z^2}} = \gamma \cdot \frac{xz}{6}$  (ii); $1 + \frac{z}{\sqrt{y^2+z^2}} + \frac{z}{\sqrt{z^2+x^2}} = \gamma \cdot \frac{xy}{6}$  (iii);

of which equating (i) through (iii) yields  $x=y=z=t \Rightarrow v(t,t,t) = \frac{1}{6}t^3=a^3 \Rightarrow t = \sqrt[3]{6}a$ , or  $(x,y,z) = (\sqrt[3]{6}a, \sqrt[3]{6}a, \sqrt[3]{6}a)$  (iv) is the critical point.  Checking the Hessian matrix of  $l$  at (iv) produces: $L(x,y,z) = \begin{bmatrix} l_{xx} && l_{xy} && l_{xz}  l_{yx} && l_{yy} && l_{yz}  l_{zx} && l_{zy} && l_{zz}\end{bmatrix}$ ;

or  $L(\sqrt[3]{6}a, \sqrt[3]{6}a, \sqrt[3]{6}a) = \begin{bmatrix} 1/\sqrt{2}\sqrt[3]{6}a && -1/2\sqrt{2}\sqrt[3]{6}a && -1/2\sqrt{2}\sqrt[3]{6}a  -1/2\sqrt{2}\sqrt[3]{6}a && 1/\sqrt{2}\sqrt[3]{6}a &&  -1/2\sqrt{2}\sqrt[3]{6}a   -1/2\sqrt{2}\sqrt[3]{6}a  && -1/2\sqrt{2}\sqrt[3]{6}a && 1/\sqrt{2}\sqrt[3]{6}a \end{bmatrix}$ ;

which has eigenvalues  $\lambda_{1} = 0, \lambda_{2}=\lambda_{3} = \frac{3}{2\sqrt{2} \sqrt[3]{6}a} \Rightarrow$  positive semi-definite (local minimum at (iv)).

Thus,  $l_{MIN} = l(\sqrt[3]{6}a, \sqrt[3]{6}a, \sqrt[3]{6}a) = \textcolor{red}{3\sqrt[3]{6}(1+\sqrt{2})a}$ .

