WLOG let  $x_1\le x_2\le\cdots\le x_n$ , then take  $a_i=(-1)^{n-i}$  for all  $i$ . Set  $d_i=x_i-x_{i-1}$  for all  $i\ge2$  and  $d_1=x_1$ . Thus, \(x_i=\sum\limits_{k=1}^id_k\).

If  $n$  is even, set  $n=2m$ , then we have
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i=1}^na_ix_i^2&=\sum_{i=1}^m\left(x_{2i}^2-x_{2i-1}^2\right)=\sum_{i=1}^md_{2i}\left(\sum_{k=1}^{2i}d_k+\sum_{k=1}^{2i-1}d_k\right)
&\ge\sum_{i=1}^md_{2i}\left(d_{2i}+2\sum_{\substack{1\le k\le 2i-1k\text{ is even}}}d_k\right)=\sum_{i=1}^md_{2i}^2+2\sum_{i=1}^md_{2i}\sum_{k=1}^{i-1}d_{2k}
&\equiv\left(\sum_{i=1}^md_{2i}\right)^2=\left[\sum_{i=1}^m(x_{2i}-x_{2i-1})\right]^2=\left(\sum_{i=1}^na_ix_i\right)^2.
\end{align*}
If  $n$  is odd, to prove the corresponding inequality, use the  $(n+1)$ -case on  $x_n$ ,  $x_{n-1}\dots$ ,  $x_1$ ,  $0$ .

